1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,921
The terminal.

2
00:00:00,921 --> 00:00:05,528
Read a shell prompt and understand commands, options, and arguments.

3
00:00:05,528 --> 00:00:10,134
The goal is clear: Identify a command and its arguments.

4
00:00:10,984 --> 00:00:12,835
Commands are precise instructions.

5
00:00:12,835 --> 00:00:19,314
A shell command usually begins with a program name, followed by options and arguments.

6
00:00:19,314 --> 00:00:25,792
Spaces separate tokens; capitalization matters; the shell does not guess which file you intended.

7
00:00:25,792 --> 00:00:34,122
In ls -lh data, ls is the command, -lh changes the display, and data is the target directory.

8
00:00:34,122 --> 00:00:38,749
Reading commands this way makes unfamiliar examples easier to adapt.

9
00:00:39,599 --> 00:00:42,668
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

10
00:00:42,668 --> 00:00:44,586
First, open a base terminal.

11
00:00:44,586 --> 00:00:46,504
Next, run the command below.

12
00:00:46,504 --> 00:00:51,491
Then, use man ls or ls --help and find the meaning of -h.

13
00:00:51,491 --> 00:00:54,559
The command shown on screen is an example.

14
00:00:54,559 --> 00:01:00,313
Adapt its path to your own working directory and verify the target before running it.

15
00:01:01,163 --> 00:01:03,322
Quality control comes before interpretation.

16
00:01:03,322 --> 00:01:05,049
The exact listing differs.

17
00:01:05,049 --> 00:01:08,503
-l adds details and -h makes sizes human-readable.

18
00:01:08,503 --> 00:01:14,547
A useful self-check is this: What role does data play in ls -lh data?

19
00:01:14,547 --> 00:01:18,433
It is the argument—the directory that ls should list.

20
00:01:19,283 --> 00:01:24,703
To recap: First, commands, options, and arguments are separate tokens.

21
00:01:24,703 --> 00:01:28,497
Next, paths and capitalization must be exact.

22
00:01:28,497 --> 00:01:36,628
Before continuing, make sure you can explain this objective: Identify a command and its arguments.
